34
00:01:28,177 --> 00:01:30,260
it's what everyone wants
to talk about these days.

35
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:32,230
I need to do some
of the older history

36
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:34,640
so that I can talk
about recent history.

37
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,700
The place that I like
to start is actually

38
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:39,220
with Claude Shannon, who
had this paper talking

39
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:41,740
about approximating--

40
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:45,280
arranging characters to
create language models.

41
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:48,910
That's probably why Anthropic
called their models Claude.

42
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:50,900
That's pretty well-known.

43
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:55,480
And a lot has happened since
these very early papers

44
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:58,310
on predicting sequences of text.

45
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:01,780
And this is largely built
on this loss function,

46
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:03,888
which is called the
autoregressive loss function.

47
00:02:03,888 --> 00:02:06,430
So if you have this training
example where you have something

48
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:10,090
like "I saw a" you're trying to
predict what comes after this,

49
00:02:10,090 --> 00:02:12,940
the whole idea is that there's
going to be one correct token

50
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:14,437
that is the correct label.

51
00:02:14,437 --> 00:02:16,270
And their training loss
is going to increase

52
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:18,700
the probability of that token
and decrease the probability

53
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:19,780
of everything else.

54
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:24,070
This very simple loss function
classifying which token to use

55
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:27,120
and actually predict
has enabled wild things.

56
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,530
And this took
another turn in 2017

57
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:32,640
when this transformer
paper was born.

58
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,912
Attention is all you need.

59
00:02:33,912 --> 00:02:35,370
Everyone here has
heard about this.

60
00:02:35,370 --> 00:02:36,950
It's a great exercise
to actually dig

61
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:39,500
into what the attention
mechanism is doing.

62
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,330
Not the focus of this talk.

63
00:02:41,330 --> 00:02:43,530
We'll quickly kind
of keep going.

64
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:45,660
In 2018, there was
three main things.

65
00:02:45,660 --> 00:02:47,240
These are slightly out of order.

66
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:48,740
ELMo was the earliest
one, which was

67
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:50,970
contextualized word embeddings.

68
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:54,200
And the same year, we also
had GPT-1 and BERT released,

69
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:58,340
which is the beginning of core
ideas on which modern language

70
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:01,040
models and transformers were
trending towards and just

71
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:03,230
getting these better
models, training on large--

72
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:06,410
large internet scaled corpora.

73
00:03:06,410 --> 00:03:07,680
BERT was a classifier.

74
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,010
GPT-1 was generating text.

75
00:03:10,010 --> 00:03:13,140
And we continue along these
trends through the years.

76
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:16,530
GPT-2 is when we started
learning about scaling laws.

77
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:19,430
And if you use orders of
magnitude more compute,

78
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:21,350
the actual test
loss will continue

79
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:25,420
to decrease in a linear fashion
with respect to the log compute.

80
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:27,250
These ideas now are
commonplace when

81
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:29,710
we talk about language models.

82
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:32,440
GPT-2 also pioneered
a lot of discussions

83
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:34,130
on releasing language models.

84
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:37,100
So GPT-2, when it
was first announced,

85
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:39,850
they were holding access
back because of the risks

86
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:40,760
of language models.

87
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:42,552
And this started a lot
of the conversations

88
00:03:42,552 --> 00:03:45,612
around what you
should or should not

89
00:03:45,612 --> 00:03:46,820
release with language models.

90
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:49,120
They eventually
actually released GPT-2,

91
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:51,830
and you can download the models
on Hugging Face and use them.

92
00:03:51,830 --> 00:03:54,247
But this is where that kind
of conversation around release

93
00:03:54,247 --> 00:03:55,300
strategies emerged.

94
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:57,640
In 2020 is when
language models really

95
00:03:57,640 --> 00:03:59,660
started to be noticeably good.

96
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:02,152
So GPT-3 is when a
lot of people were

97
00:04:02,152 --> 00:04:04,610
like, whoa, this can actually
do really interesting things.

98
00:04:04,610 --> 00:04:06,940
If I create a really
clever prompt,

99
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:09,670
figure out how to give it
my information correctly,

100
00:04:09,670 --> 00:04:11,890
and GPT-3 could
do a ton of things

101
00:04:11,890 --> 00:04:14,500
with this few-shot or
multi-shot learning, which

102
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:17,380
is when you give it a few
examples in the prompt

103
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:20,170
and then ask it to do
another rendition of it.

104
00:04:20,170 --> 00:04:23,330
And with this power
came many harms.

105
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:25,490
And this is a
discussion of, what

106
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:27,920
are the risks of
releasing language models?

107
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,200
What types of groups
will be hurt by this?

108
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:33,620
Very important problems
that culminated in 2021

109
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:37,550
with the stochastic parrots
paper, which is arguing about

110
00:04:37,550 --> 00:04:40,860
whether or not language models
can be too big, is in the title.

111
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:44,360
But it's really a critique
on how we should be

112
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:46,200
thinking about language models.

113
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:47,880
What are the limits of them?

114
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:49,760
Are they actually
doing the things?

115
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:51,620
Like, are they actually
thinking or doing

116
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:52,830
any of these human things?

117
00:04:52,830 --> 00:04:55,700
Or are they just kind of
following patterns in the data?

118
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:59,660
And then just the year
after, this is like the--

119
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:01,483
it's like the tragedy
of syntactic parrots

120
00:05:01,483 --> 00:05:02,900
because no one
talks about it now,

121
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:05,720
is that ChatGPT
came the year later

122
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:08,180
and totally reshaped the whole
narrative around language

123
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:09,380
models one more time.

124
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:11,610
And this is really
where we start

125
00:05:11,610 --> 00:05:16,070
today's talk, is like, how
does this idea of alignment

126
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:17,610
emerge in ChatGPT?

127
00:05:17,610 --> 00:05:19,730
And then what
happens after this?

128
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:22,070
So the question that
I ask myself is like--

129
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:27,050
or I tell a lot of people is,
can ChatGPT exist without RLHF?

130
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:29,610
And what we saw in
the release day--

131
00:05:29,610 --> 00:05:32,840
so if you go back and read the
actual OpenAI blog about RLHF,

132
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,940
they list all these limitations.

133
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:37,520
But they say that RLHF
was an important tool

134
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:38,600
