00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:43,646
It's one big ecosystem.

231
00:09:43,646 --> 00:09:47,240

232
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:49,370
Another thing that's
not fun but I'm

233
00:09:49,370 --> 00:09:53,030
going to do for the sake
of flag posting, no one

234
00:09:53,030 --> 00:09:55,280
really likes listening
to definitions.

235
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:58,127
Here are some things that
you'll hear thrown around.

236
00:09:58,127 --> 00:10:00,710
This isn't even all of them when
talking about, quote unquote,

237
00:10:00,710 --> 00:10:01,650
"alignment."

238
00:10:01,650 --> 00:10:03,500
I have here, like,
alignment I've

239
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,780
defined as a general
notion of training a model

240
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:07,930
to mirror a user's desires.

241
00:10:07,930 --> 00:10:11,708
Really, with any loss
function, it's not restricted.

242
00:10:11,708 --> 00:10:14,000
So there's a difference
between instruction fine-tuning

243
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,670
and supervised fine-tuning.

244
00:10:15,670 --> 00:10:18,080
Instruction fine-tuning
is about trying

245
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:20,270
to get a model that
will respond to queries,

246
00:10:20,270 --> 00:10:23,300
formatted instructions, while
supervised fine-tuning is

247
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:26,280
more about learning a
specific tasks capabilities.

248
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:28,280
These get interchanged
all the time.

249
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:29,790
Like, that's OK.

250
00:10:29,790 --> 00:10:31,700
It's good to know that
they're different.

251
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:35,670
And then two more ones
that I need to touch on

252
00:10:35,670 --> 00:10:38,250
and we could go on even
longer is reinforcement

253
00:10:38,250 --> 00:10:39,700
learning from human feedback.

254
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:41,460
There's this
multi-stage process.

255
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:44,910
It's a specific tool
for aligning ML models

256
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:46,860
to human data.

257
00:10:46,860 --> 00:10:50,790
It's a class of tools, so it has
some learn a preference model.

258
00:10:50,790 --> 00:10:52,575
And then you extract
information from it.

259
00:10:52,575 --> 00:10:54,450
So there are so many
different ways to do it.

260
00:10:54,450 --> 00:10:55,660
It's really an approach.

261
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:57,450
And then there's a
term that I'm trying

262
00:10:57,450 --> 00:11:00,540
to grow, which is preference
fine-tuning, which

263
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:03,560
could encompass RLHF
methods, like PPO.

264
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:06,060
But there's a question of, how
do we differentiate something

265
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:08,670
like direct preference
optimization, which

266
00:11:08,670 --> 00:11:12,330
doesn't use an RL
optimizer from all of RLHF?

267
00:11:12,330 --> 00:11:14,610
And I'll come back
to this, but it's

268
00:11:14,610 --> 00:11:16,512
good to have some common
grounds to build on

269
00:11:16,512 --> 00:11:18,720
because I might be going
through some of these things

270
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:19,345
pretty quickly.

271
00:11:19,345 --> 00:11:26,940

272
00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:29,880
This is a chapter that
I cover in one slide

273
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:32,370
because it's really
tapping into a lot

274
00:11:32,370 --> 00:11:34,010
of different personal stories.

275
00:11:34,010 --> 00:11:38,640
It's hard to retell
how crazy things were

276
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:40,390
when ChatGPT dropped.

277
00:11:40,390 --> 00:11:43,290
People were not really
losing their mind,

278
00:11:43,290 --> 00:11:45,720
but there was a
lot of uncertainty

279
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:50,970
on what the future held,
especially that it was clear

280
00:11:50,970 --> 00:11:52,470
that language models
were important.

281
00:11:52,470 --> 00:11:53,900
But it is not clear--

282
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:55,650
there's a lot of
articles on like--

283
00:11:55,650 --> 00:11:57,690
titled, we're going
to reproduce open

284
00:11:57,690 --> 00:12:02,850
ChatGPT, which you can't really
have an open model that does

285
00:12:02,850 --> 00:12:04,920
what a closed product does.

286
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:07,560
There's a difference between
model weights and this product

287
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:10,500
that ChatGPT represents, but
there's so much excitement

288
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:12,840
that everyone is saying they're
going to do these things

289
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:15,630
and trying to figure out the
right coalitions for actually

290
00:12:15,630 --> 00:12:16,660
doing so.

291
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:18,680
And it's interesting.

292
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:21,240
This delay is this
land grab, where people

293
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:22,530
are learning the basic things.

294
00:12:22,530 --> 00:12:24,600
Like, what is red-teaming?

295
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:26,530
What is the difference
between a dialogue

296
00:12:26,530 --> 00:12:28,780
agent and a predictive
language model?

297
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:30,410
What tools should we use?

298
00:12:30,410 --> 00:12:34,370
And everything follows from here
with what people are building.

299
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:36,732
But like, personally, I just
remember multiple meetings

300
00:12:36,732 --> 00:12:38,690
where people were like,
yeah, you should do it.

301
00:12:38,690 --> 00:12:40,400
You should go try to
build open ChatGPT.

302
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:43,120
And when you look
back, that goal is just

303
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:45,520
so wild that so many
people are just going like,

304
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:48,380
we need to build this
thing in the open source.

305
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:49,900
And it's just like,
it doesn't even

306
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:51,700
make sense because
you can't open source

307
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:54,375
a whole system that way.

308
00:12:54,375 --> 00:12:57,533
But there are some
things that make a bit--

309
00:12:57,533 --> 00:12:58,950
like, this makes
a lot more sense,

310
00:12:58,950 --> 00:13:02,470
which is when things start to
get grounded in actual models.

311
00:13:02,470 --> 00:13:05,400
So the first Llama suite
was released in, I think,

312
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:06,250
in February.

313
00:13:06,250 --> 00:13:08,640
I have the date in
some notes somewhere.

314
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:11,370
And then these instruction
tuned models started to show up

315
00:13:11,370 --> 00:13:13,740
on this first Llama model.

316
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:15,570
The first one to really
crack the narrative

317
00:13:15,570 --> 00:13:17,850
was this Alpaca
model, and it did

318
00:13:17,850 --> 00:13:21,430
a bunch of things that
still are used today.

319
00:13:21,430 --> 00:13:25,530
So this was trained on 52,000
self-constructed style data

320
00:13:25,530 --> 00:13:27,110
distilled from text-davinci-003.

321
00:13:27,110 --> 00:13:28,360
There's a lot in the sentence.

322
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:29,970
I'll say what
self-construct means.

323
00:13:29,970 --> 00:13:33,660
But this wasn't even data
generated from ChatGPT.

324
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:36,490
It was generated from one
of OpenAI's API models.

325
00:13:36,490 --> 00:13:39,060
So if we talk about--

326
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:42,570
this is all on how to apply
instruction fine-tuning.

327
00:13:42,570 --> 00:13:44,910
And this thing I mentioned
on the definition slide.

328
00:13:44,910 --> 00:13:48,300
But really, it's about making
a model that will respond

329
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:50,320
to specific styles of inputs.

330
00:13:50,320 --> 00:13:52,410
What often happens at
a technical level here

331
00:13:52,410 --> 00:13:54,930
is that the model is learning
to integrate something

332
00:13:54,930 --> 00:13:57,390
called a chat
template, the ability

333
00:13:57,390 --> 00:13:58,630
to include system prompts.

334
00:13:58,630 --> 00:14:00,297
So you want to tell--
you want the model

335
00:14:00,297 --> 00:14:01,270
to know it is an agent.

336
00:14:01,270 --> 00:14:04,240
You want the model to
know what day it is.

337
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:04,740
Excuse me.

338
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:06,450
You can do this in the system
prompt, which is something

339
00:14:06,450 --> 00:14:07,550
that the user doesn't see.

340
00:14:07,550 --> 00:14:10,437
But it steers the
behavior of the model.

341
00:14:10,437 --> 00:14:12,020
And instruction
tuning is really where

342
00:14:12,020 --> 00:14:15,500
we make the model capable
of having these behaviors.

343
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:17,240
But the question is
like, what data are

344
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:19,550
we training this behavior on?

345
00:14:19,550 --> 00:14:25,130
So the most common example
is you continue training

346
00:14:25,130 --> 00:14:26,930
with this autoregressive
loss function

347
00:14:26,930 --> 00:14:28,890
on question-answer pairs.

348
00:14:28,890 --> 00:14:31,170
So it's like, what
is a transformer?

349
00:14:31,170 --> 00:14:33,980
And then the language model
will predict an answer.

350
00:14:33,980 --> 00:14:35,270
Could be from stack overflow.

351
00:14:35,270 --> 00:14:36,630
It could be something else.

352
00:14:36,630 --> 00:14:39,150
And this example is human data.

353
00:14:39,150 --> 00:14:43,010
But what made Alpaca and a
lot of these early models,

354
00:14:43,010 --> 00:14:45,590
and even today, really
popular and accessible

355
00:14:45,590 --> 00:14:48,350
is by using data
to answer questions

356
00:14:48,350 --> 00:14:50,730
that is generated by an AI.

357
00:14:50,730 --> 00:14:55,670
So this is where the idea of
self-instruct data comes in.

358
00:14:55,670 --> 00:15:00,990
Self-instruct was a paper
from Allen AI and UDub in 2022

359
00:15:00,990 --> 00:15:04,740
before ChatGPT, where
essentially the idea

360
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:08,430
is, how do we expand on the
distribution and instruction

361
00:15:08,430 --> 00:15:11,250
data that we have, this training
data for fine-tuning a language

362
00:15:11,250 --> 00:15:14,370
model without getting
more humans in the loop?

363
00:15:14,370 --> 00:15:17,520
So what you really
have to do is you

364
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:21,150
start with some high-quality,
often human prompts.

365
00:15:21,150 --> 00:15:24,510
And then what we now see as
more common practice today

366
00:15:24,510 --> 00:15:28,660
but was very new then is asking
a stronger language model.

367
00:15:28,660 --> 00:15:31,860
Create a list of prompts
that are similar to this

368
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:33,240
but still diverse.

369
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:35,850
And then once you have
a list of prompts,

370
00:15:35,850 --> 00:15:38,400
you can use ChatGPT
or another model

371
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:40,020
to actually generate
completions.

372
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:42,900
Because then what you
have is a really big list

373
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:45,265
of question-answer
pairs, but you

374
00:15:45,265 --> 00:15:47,640
don't need to go through the
bottleneck of getting humans

375
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:49,410
to sit down and
write all of them.

376
00:15:49,410 --> 00:15:51,540
So what Alpaca was really--

377
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:56,250
why Alpaca worked is because
of realizing this and taking

378
00:15:56,250 --> 00:15:57,927
in this better
model from OpenAI.

379
00:15:57,927 --> 00:15:59,760
So you can see this
figure here on the right

380
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:02,850
is from the Alpaca paper or
blog post, one of the two.

381
00:16:02,850 --> 00:16:05,760
They took this model from
OpenAI and they asked

382
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:08,620
it to generate more tasks.

383
00:16:08,620 --> 00:16:12,490
So they had 175 to start, and
then they ended up with over

384
00:16:12,490 --> 00:16:14,272
50,000 tasks.

385
00:16:14,272 --> 00:16:15,730
And they also
generated completions

386
00:16:15,730 --> 00:16:17,410
from this OpenAI model.

387
00:16:17,410 --> 00:16:21,430
And then what they did is they
took these Meta weights that

388
00:16:21,430 --> 00:16:24,100
had just come out, and they
instruction fine-tuning them.

389
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:25,760
And then you end up with Alpaca.

390
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:27,667
This is a pattern.

391
00:16:27,667 --> 00:16:29,500
This is a pattern that
we've seen many times

392
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:32,920
with Alpaca, which is
essentially, you take--

393
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:37,067
you generate some data from
a stronger language model,

394
00:16:37,067 --> 00:16:38,150
and you fine-tuning on it.

395
00:16:38,150 --> 00:16:40,900
It sounds so obvious today,
but this was the first model

396
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:44,160
to actually release this.

397
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:46,110
I can now see
questions coming in.

398
00:16:46,110 --> 00:16:49,900
I'll answer the ones that are
clarifying and stuff like this,

399
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:51,030
so Thanks for asking them.

400
00:16:51,030 --> 00:16:54,110
And we can come back
to more at the end.

401
00:16:54,110 --> 00:16:55,940
Once Alpaca happened,
it felt like there

402
00:16:55,940 --> 00:16:57,240
was a new model every week.

403
00:16:57,240 --> 00:16:59,000
The second model was Vicuna.

404
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,550
And really, what
they changed was

405
00:17:01,550 --> 00:17:07,560
they added new sources of
prompts to the distribution.

406
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:10,460
So you can see that
I say ShareGPT.

407
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:13,339
They also introduced the
idea of LLM-as-a-judge,

408
00:17:13,339 --> 00:17:17,359
which is now obvious from a lot
of their later evaluation work.

409
00:17:17,359 --> 00:17:21,319
But let's talk about why
ShareGPT was so interesting.

410
00:17:21,319 --> 00:17:25,430
So ShareGPT was one
of the only data

411
00:17:25,430 --> 00:17:31,040
sets that got open
language model builders, so

412
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:36,110
people like me, prompts that
were similar to what people

413
00:17:36,110 --> 00:17:38,000
were asking ChatGPT.

414
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,870
So what was happening was you
would install this browser

415
00:17:41,870 --> 00:17:47,300
plug-in, and it would let you
share your prompts from ChatGPT

416
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:48,810
on Twitter or whatever.

417
00:17:48,810 --> 00:17:51,770
So it was making it easier
to share the prompts before--

418
00:17:51,770 --> 00:17:55,560
in your conversations, before
OpenAI made a tool to do this.

419
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:59,340
And now there's
this legal gray area

420
00:17:59,340 --> 00:18:01,740
over the data set because
most of these data sets

421
00:18:01,740 --> 00:18:04,260
are unlicensed, and they
were created without consent.

422
00:18:04,260 --> 00:18:06,220
Or they were released
without consent.

423
00:18:06,220 --> 00:18:09,900
So there's a legal question of
whether or not people should

424
00:18:09,900 --> 00:18:11,350
be trading on this data.

425
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:13,230
But the fact of the
matter is that ShareGPT

426
00:18:13,230 --> 00:18:16,290
was really important to this
acceleration in progress

427
00:18:16,290 --> 00:18:19,890
on fine-tuning models
because the diversity of data

428
00:18:19,890 --> 00:18:21,600
is just so much
stronger than what

429
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:25,570
people were going to get from
this Alpaca self-instruct idea.

430
00:18:25,570 --> 00:18:27,330
And it set the bar much higher.

431
00:18:27,330 --> 00:18:31,018
It's only today in the last
few months or six months

432
00:18:31,018 --> 00:18:33,060
for some of them that were
getting data sets that

433
00:18:33,060 --> 00:18:33,940
can replace these.

434
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:37,530
So you see I mentioned
LMSYS-Chat-1M,

435
00:18:37,530 --> 00:18:40,320
which is just a million
conversations from ChatBotArena,

436
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:44,130
which took a lot of work to
clean out personal information;

437
00:18:44,130 --> 00:18:46,290
and then a project from
the Allen Institute

438
00:18:46,290 --> 00:18:50,640
of AI, which is WildChat, which
is really similar to ShareGPT.

439
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:53,220
But the users were giving
consent at the start

440
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:55,500
that their data was going
to be collected and released

441
00:18:55,500 --> 00:18:58,680
in exchange for using a
language model for free.

442
00:18:58,680 --> 00:19:02,670
So there's a lot of
happenstance in the story where

443
00:19:02,670 --> 00:19:05,250
something like this,
which is legally gray--

444
00:19:05,250 --> 00:19:09,510
and the data is still on Hugging
face, but it looks kind of odd--

445
00:19:09,510 --> 00:19:12,990
where these little things helped
enable the ecosystem, even

446
00:19:12,990 --> 00:19:14,775
though, looking
back, it's like, oh,

447
00:19:14,775 --> 00:19:16,567
we don't know if that
should have happened.

448
00:19:16,567 --> 00:19:20,260

449
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:25,070
Following Vicuna is one called
Koala, also from Berkeley.

450
00:19:25,070 --> 00:19:27,965
And look at the timeframes.

451
00:19:27,965 --> 00:19:29,590
It's pretty obvious
that a lot of these

452
00:19:29,590 --> 00:19:31,150
were developed
concurrently, and then

453
00:19:31,150 --> 00:19:34,120
their release dates just happen
to be slightly different.

454
00:19:34,120 --> 00:19:38,280
Koala is mostly known for having
a different diverse set of data

455
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:38,780
sets.

456
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:40,690
They use some from Alpaca.

457
00:19:40,690 --> 00:19:43,900
They use some from
ShareGPT again.

458
00:19:43,900 --> 00:19:46,420
They also use Anthropic
data that has been released,

459
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:49,160
and they had some human
evaluation from grad students.

460
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:51,250
So this just added
more data diversity,

461
00:19:51,250 --> 00:19:53,397
and the evaluations
weren't necessarily better.

462
00:19:53,397 --> 00:19:55,480
But it was an important
model that a lot of people

463
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:58,960
noticed just from bringing
back up these new data

464
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:02,913
sets that had been in the
literature from years prior.

465
00:20:02,913 --> 00:20:05,080
Something that you might
ask looking at these slides

466
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:07,490
is that it's like, why
weight differences?

467
00:20:07,490 --> 00:20:10,480
I have all these slides,
like, weight diff to LLaMA 7B,

468
00:20:10,480 --> 00:20:12,100
weight diff.

469
00:20:12,100 --> 00:20:14,050
Essentially, when
Llama was released,

470
00:20:14,050 --> 00:20:15,910
it was released
as research only.

471
00:20:15,910 --> 00:20:18,850
And you had to-- and it was
distributed to researchers

472
00:20:18,850 --> 00:20:21,610
upon request, and the
license prohibited people

473
00:20:21,610 --> 00:20:23,750
from updating Llama
1 to Hugging Face.

474
00:20:23,750 --> 00:20:25,660
So it's this
annoying phase where,

475
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:27,370
in order to use a
model on Hugging Face,

476
00:20:27,370 --> 00:20:28,690
you had to clone it.

477
00:20:28,690 --> 00:20:32,710
And then you had to run a script
to convert it with this delta

478
00:20:32,710 --> 00:20:35,990
into the new model in
order to actually use it.

479
00:20:35,990 --> 00:20:39,460
So this is a really frustrating
phase from a user perspective

480
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:41,950
because it just made
experimentation have one more

481
00:20:41,950 --> 00:20:43,390
barrier to entry.

482
00:20:43,390 --> 00:20:45,790
And thankfully, it was
changed with Llama 2,

483
00:20:45,790 --> 00:20:47,770
but it was really
something that many people

484
00:20:47,770 --> 00:20:49,370
dealt with at the time.

485
00:20:49,370 --> 00:20:52,420
And we now today see
different license restrictions

486
00:20:52,420 --> 00:20:55,130
on how Llama is used.

487
00:20:55,130 --> 00:20:57,460
I mean, the Llama
3 released today--

488
00:20:57,460 --> 00:21:00,460
essentially, if I fine-tuning
a model for my research

489
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:04,050
and I release it, too, Llama
3 needs to be in the name.

490
00:21:04,050 --> 00:21:07,100
So if I want to release
a new Tulu model,

491
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:10,625
it would have to be
Llama 3, Tulu 4 DPO.

492
00:21:10,625 --> 00:21:12,500
It's like, the namings
are going to be crazy.

493
00:21:12,500 --> 00:21:14,000
But there's always
been restrictions

494
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:18,740
on using Llama weights
or how you share them.

495
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:21,710
And the final model that
I group into this batch

496
00:21:21,710 --> 00:21:24,270
of this real first
swing was Dolly.

497
00:21:24,270 --> 00:21:27,412
So Dolly was fine-tuning
from a different base model.

498
00:21:27,412 --> 00:21:29,120
It was fine-tuning
from the Pythia models

499
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:32,060
from a Luther, which were
a suite of early scaling

500
00:21:32,060 --> 00:21:36,060
experiments from EleutherAI,
which is still used extensively.

501
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:38,000
But they added some
human-written data

502
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:42,025
to the loop, which is really
important because almost all

503
00:21:42,025 --> 00:21:43,400
of the projects
that I'll mention

504
00:21:43,400 --> 00:21:47,090
today talk about synthetic data
or data derived from OpenAI.

505
00:21:47,090 --> 00:21:50,090
There's only a few of them that
actually added new human data

506
00:21:50,090 --> 00:21:53,240
to the loop, and this is what
everyone remembered Dolly for.

507
00:21:53,240 --> 00:21:55,250
And a lot of its
performance limitations

508
00:21:55,250 --> 00:21:57,080
were probably from
the base model Pythia,

509
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:00,290
which is trained in a time
where this type of inference

510
00:22:00,290 --> 00:22:02,610
that people expect
wasn't as popular.

511
00:22:02,610 --> 00:22:03,740
And it was before--

512
00:22:03,740 --> 00:22:08,630
the scaling laws were
thought of differently.

513
00:22:08,630 --> 00:22:10,460
You can see through
these where we're

514
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:13,550
going to start with different
model sizes and different MT

