00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:16,770
I'll talk about what MT
bench is in a few slides.

517
00:22:16,770 --> 00:22:18,210
It's an evaluation tool.

518
00:22:18,210 --> 00:22:22,610
And this is really just to
ground you on what the--

519
00:22:22,610 --> 00:22:25,202
how the scores would
change over time.

520
00:22:25,202 --> 00:22:26,660
So I have these
throughout the talk

521
00:22:26,660 --> 00:22:28,340
as we go through
different models

522
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:33,680
just to show how the scores
continue to progress over time

523
00:22:33,680 --> 00:22:36,350
from one small change to another
as the community gets better

524
00:22:36,350 --> 00:22:38,860
at these things.

525
00:22:38,860 --> 00:22:40,430
While I was talking
about human data,

526
00:22:40,430 --> 00:22:43,290
so remember, Dolly is
all about human data.

527
00:22:43,290 --> 00:22:49,680
Probably still the single
busiest human coordination

528
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:52,570
project for data generation
was OpenAssistant.

529
00:22:52,570 --> 00:22:55,470
I think it's easy now if
you get into fine-tuning

530
00:22:55,470 --> 00:22:57,780
to see the open system
data set and not

531
00:22:57,780 --> 00:23:02,940
realize how important it was
to the process of alignment

532
00:23:02,940 --> 00:23:04,110
in this whole summer.

533
00:23:04,110 --> 00:23:05,793
And it is still used today.

534
00:23:05,793 --> 00:23:07,710
So essentially, there's
this quote on the top.

535
00:23:07,710 --> 00:23:11,460
But the leaders ran
a community project

536
00:23:11,460 --> 00:23:13,870
to generate
human-written prompts

537
00:23:13,870 --> 00:23:18,090
and these
human-written responses

538
00:23:18,090 --> 00:23:20,620
in many different
languages with rating them.

539
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:22,710
So you could use it
as preferences with--

540
00:23:22,710 --> 00:23:25,320
the slide has a bug,
where it's like,

541
00:23:25,320 --> 00:23:28,500
it has over 10,000
annotated trees and over

542
00:23:28,500 --> 00:23:30,490
1,000 volunteers.

543
00:23:30,490 --> 00:23:32,430
This is still used
extensively today,

544
00:23:32,430 --> 00:23:34,000
will come up again in the talk.

545
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:35,650
They also released models.

546
00:23:35,650 --> 00:23:39,470
So the first one of the first
model used on Hugging Chat,

547
00:23:39,470 --> 00:23:41,220
which I don't remember
the launch date of,

548
00:23:41,220 --> 00:23:43,190
was an OpenAssistant model.

549
00:23:43,190 --> 00:23:46,930
So OpenAssistant was probably
the first majorly successful

550
00:23:46,930 --> 00:23:50,770
project of the era, and the
data set is still used today.

551
00:23:50,770 --> 00:23:52,780
Where I will end
this talk is saying

