00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:22,980
except for the data set,
which is now popular.

562
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:24,640
They got PPO to work.

563
00:24:24,640 --> 00:24:27,100
They had some human
evaluations that were solid.

564
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:28,840
It was a good chat model.

565
00:24:28,840 --> 00:24:33,030
It wasn't out of distribution,
but CarperAI was really

566
00:24:33,030 --> 00:24:34,720
ahead at the time.

567
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:36,840
And then it seems like
priorities kind of shifted

568
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:38,410
from stability.

569
00:24:38,410 --> 00:24:41,610
But it's important to know the
extent by which there were still

570
00:24:41,610 --> 00:24:45,600
some players who knew how to
do RLHF really early on, even

571
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:47,000
though there were pretty rare.

572
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,860

573
00:24:51,860 --> 00:24:52,682
This is the last--

574
00:24:52,682 --> 00:24:54,140
this is the last
slide of this kind

575
00:24:54,140 --> 00:24:56,180
of first chapter on
instruction tuning,

576
00:24:56,180 --> 00:24:59,900
was the idea of
QLoRA, which unlocked

577
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:03,590
a whole new bunch of
players into actually

578
00:25:03,590 --> 00:25:05,250
being able to fine-tune models.

579
00:25:05,250 --> 00:25:09,110
So for the quick
60-second overview,

580
00:25:09,110 --> 00:25:11,600
LoRA stands for Low
Rank Adaptation,

581
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:14,558
which is the idea of
can freeze some model--

582
00:25:14,558 --> 00:25:16,100
you freeze most of
the model weights,

583
00:25:16,100 --> 00:25:19,490
and you add new weights to
specific layers that you can

584
00:25:19,490 --> 00:25:23,040
then fine-tuning as if you were
fine-tuning the whole model.

585
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:25,790
You use the same approach
of instruction data

586
00:25:25,790 --> 00:25:29,485
with question answering, but
it takes much less memory.

587
00:25:29,485 --> 00:25:31,820
QLoRA was a technique
that built upon this

588
00:25:31,820 --> 00:25:34,700
by adding very specific
quantization and GPU

589
00:25:34,700 --> 00:25:36,920
tricks to make it
so that it memory

590
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:40,290
requirements to fine-tune
models was even lower.

591
00:25:40,290 --> 00:25:44,060
Tim Dettmers and team also
released this Guanaco model

592
00:25:44,060 --> 00:25:49,020
with it, which was another
big step up in performance

593
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:49,780
of these models.

594
00:25:49,780 --> 00:25:51,940
I have a few more slides
on it, on the methods.

595
00:25:51,940 --> 00:25:55,440
So you can see on the right this
difference, full fine-tuning

596
00:25:55,440 --> 00:25:56,130
and LoRA.

597
00:25:56,130 --> 00:25:58,830
They look similar, where LoRA
you have fewer parameters,

598
00:25:58,830 --> 00:26:00,210
as what the smaller shapes mean.

599
00:26:00,210 --> 00:26:03,632
And QLoRA, they
quantize the base model

600
00:26:03,632 --> 00:26:05,340
that you're propagating
gradients through

601
00:26:05,340 --> 00:26:07,920
to save most of the memory.

602
00:26:07,920 --> 00:26:10,770
So this is an
approximation of, if you're

603
00:26:10,770 --> 00:26:14,100
fine-tuning different model
sizes on the top, so 7 billion,

604
00:26:14,100 --> 00:26:17,130
13 billion, 30 billion,
with full fine-tuning

605
00:26:17,130 --> 00:26:19,110
different amount of bits
but full fine-tuning

606
00:26:19,110 --> 00:26:21,430
versus LoRa versus QLoRA.

607
00:26:21,430 --> 00:26:25,200
And you can see,
for reference, one

608
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:28,860
A100 GPU has about 80
gigabytes of memory.

609
00:26:28,860 --> 00:26:31,350
And these are really
hard GPUs to get.

610
00:26:31,350 --> 00:26:33,360
Plenty of consumer
GPUs will only

611
00:26:33,360 --> 00:26:36,880
have 24 to 32
gigabytes of memory.

612
00:26:36,880 --> 00:26:40,260
So you need to use these QLoRA
techniques to actually get

613
00:26:40,260 --> 00:26:45,210
the ability to fine-tune models
at the 7 or 13 billion parameter

614
00:26:45,210 --> 00:26:45,710
size.

615
00:26:45,710 --> 00:26:48,550

616
00:26:48,550 --> 00:26:50,770
And like, Guanaco
did this, and they

617
00:26:50,770 --> 00:26:54,160
released 33 billion and
65 billion parameter Llama

618
00:26:54,160 --> 00:26:56,380
fine-tunes, which
were clear steps up

619
00:26:56,380 --> 00:26:58,790
in the kind of state
of the art at the time.

620
00:26:58,790 --> 00:27:02,830
And they also figured out ways
to filter this OpenAssistant

621
00:27:02,830 --> 00:27:04,810
data set that I
mentioned, and this kind

622
00:27:04,810 --> 00:27:06,520
of filtered version
of OpenAssistant

623
00:27:06,520 --> 00:27:09,910
is what is still
most popular today.

624
00:27:09,910 --> 00:27:12,160
I'm going to pause and
skim through the questions

625
00:27:12,160 --> 00:27:14,030
and see if there's
anything on that section.

626
00:27:14,030 --> 00:27:18,270
And if not, I'll save the
relevant ones for later.

627
00:27:18,270 --> 00:27:19,510
OK, I'm going to keep going.

628
00:27:19,510 --> 00:27:22,380
They're great questions,
and I appreciate them.

629
00:27:22,380 --> 00:27:25,860
But they're mostly
not specific enough

630
00:27:25,860 --> 00:27:28,100
where it's worth the digression.

631
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:31,550
This chapter 2 phase
is really where

632
00:27:31,550 --> 00:27:36,318
it seemed like things were a
little bit slower on the ground.

633
00:27:36,318 --> 00:27:38,360
But when we look back at
a lot of the things that

634
00:27:38,360 --> 00:27:41,990
came out of this time, like
the DPO paper was in this era.

635
00:27:41,990 --> 00:27:44,490
Everyone read it, but we didn't
know what to do with it yet.

636
00:27:44,490 --> 00:27:48,850
And the new evaluations
are still really used.

637
00:27:48,850 --> 00:27:50,890
Transitioning in,
setting the scene

638
00:27:50,890 --> 00:27:54,790
for being not sure if
things work, a lot of people

639
00:27:54,790 --> 00:27:58,000
are continuing to try to build
on these LoRA methods and QLoRA

640
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:58,622
methods.

641
00:27:58,622 --> 00:28:00,580
I remember a lot of
excitement at Hugging Face,

642
00:28:00,580 --> 00:28:03,340
where we were setting
up our RLHF pipeline,

643
00:28:03,340 --> 00:28:07,390
where we could do RLHF on
7 billion parameter models.

644
00:28:07,390 --> 00:28:10,010
And we could maybe do
it on a consumer GPU.

645
00:28:10,010 --> 00:28:12,700
It was really cool to
see the loss going down.

646
00:28:12,700 --> 00:28:15,340
It's great to bring more
people into the space.

647
00:28:15,340 --> 00:28:17,380
But weeks and weeks would go by.

648
00:28:17,380 --> 00:28:20,500
And they're like, why
has no one picked up

649
00:28:20,500 --> 00:28:22,330
what we released
in the blog post

650
00:28:22,330 --> 00:28:24,500
and trained a really
good model with it?

651
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:30,460
And the kind of consensus now
is that these LoRA methods just

652
00:28:30,460 --> 00:28:33,730
have some sort of weird
limitation in how you use them

653
00:28:33,730 --> 00:28:37,150
or how the gradients flow
that make it much, much harder

654
00:28:37,150 --> 00:28:38,870
to get a really good model out.

655
00:28:38,870 --> 00:28:41,320
If you only have a
certain number of GPUs,

656
00:28:41,320 --> 00:28:44,360
such that LoRA is your only
option, definitely use it.

657
00:28:44,360 --> 00:28:47,420
But for people that have more
GPUs, figuring out how to scale

658
00:28:47,420 --> 00:28:49,520
is normally a better
solution than just

659
00:28:49,520 --> 00:28:51,440
using something like
LoRA that fits in

660
00:28:51,440 --> 00:28:55,300
is easier in the short term.

661
00:28:55,300 --> 00:29:00,010
Another defining moment of this
era was the Llama 2 backlash.

662
00:29:00,010 --> 00:29:01,600
I'm guessing some
people remember

663
00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:05,440
this, which is like, the famous
line was people ask Llama

664
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:06,910
how to kill a Python process.

665
00:29:06,910 --> 00:29:08,260
And it would say, no.

666
00:29:08,260 --> 00:29:12,010
And this really started a
whole bunch of new discussions

667
00:29:12,010 --> 00:29:15,130
around what kind
of alignment means

668
00:29:15,130 --> 00:29:17,840
or what model should
or should not do.

669
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:21,580
Here's an example from a
paper for a safety evaluation

670
00:29:21,580 --> 00:29:25,180
test set called XSTest,
and it's just like,

671
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:27,100
should chat models be safe?

672
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:30,470
Or should they follow the
instructions that I want?

673
00:29:30,470 --> 00:29:32,290
And this is a
fundamental question.

674
00:29:32,290 --> 00:29:33,790
It'll differ by
organization, and it

675
00:29:33,790 --> 00:29:35,570
will differ by individual.

676
00:29:35,570 --> 00:29:38,973
And this is the point where
this became very serious

677
00:29:38,973 --> 00:29:40,390
and something that
people actually

678
00:29:40,390 --> 00:29:44,960
had to reckon with because there
were models that were actively--

679
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:48,700
people were really disagreeing
with the specific take.

680
00:29:48,700 --> 00:29:50,830
I don't have any
clear solution to it.

681
00:29:50,830 --> 00:29:52,480
But one of the
things that led to

682
00:29:52,480 --> 00:29:55,450
is this idea of
uncensored models.

683
00:29:55,450 --> 00:30:01,120
It's a really popular
category on Hugging Face

684
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:03,910
right now, where the idea is you
remove filtering-- so if we're

685
00:30:03,910 --> 00:30:06,610
using synthetic data
and ask a language model

686
00:30:06,610 --> 00:30:09,432
a question-- like, if I ask
ChatGPT how to make a bomb,

687
00:30:09,432 --> 00:30:10,640
it's going to say, I'm sorry.

688
00:30:10,640 --> 00:30:11,630
I'm a language model.

689
00:30:11,630 --> 00:30:12,730
I shouldn't make this.

690
00:30:12,730 --> 00:30:14,560
And the idea of
uncensored models

691
00:30:14,560 --> 00:30:18,300
is to remove those
points from our--

692
00:30:18,300 --> 00:30:21,680
remove those points from
our fine-tuning data set.

693
00:30:21,680 --> 00:30:23,680
I think there's a lot of
confusion over the name

694
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:25,540
because language
models were never--

695
00:30:25,540 --> 00:30:28,870
at this stage, really aren't
censored to begin with.

696
00:30:28,870 --> 00:30:31,150
But it's really
that the data set

697
00:30:31,150 --> 00:30:32,820
and the method for
creating these data

698
00:30:32,820 --> 00:30:36,220
sets needed more
filtering, or they needed

699
00:30:36,220 --> 00:30:38,030
some way of becoming unbiased.

700
00:30:38,030 --> 00:30:41,110
So there's a lot
of people now that

701
00:30:41,110 --> 00:30:44,060
only build models to try
to make them unbiased

702
00:30:44,060 --> 00:30:45,422
against any sort of refusal.

703
00:30:45,422 --> 00:30:47,630
A refusal is when you ask
a language model something,

704
00:30:47,630 --> 00:30:48,720
and it says, no.

705
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:54,170
And this goes on today, and this
came out of this Llama 2 thing.

706
00:30:54,170 --> 00:30:56,870
But otherwise, this
is a transition period

707
00:30:56,870 --> 00:31:00,530
where there is a lot of good,
solid models being trained.

708
00:31:00,530 --> 00:31:02,840
But either they didn't have
a lot of documentation.

709
00:31:02,840 --> 00:31:04,730
They didn't have the
right release team

710
00:31:04,730 --> 00:31:06,840
to splash as big as
they should have.

711
00:31:06,840 --> 00:31:09,020
The methods were
complicated to implement

712
00:31:09,020 --> 00:31:10,080
or something like this.

713
00:31:10,080 --> 00:31:12,380
So I could run through
these, and I remember

714
00:31:12,380 --> 00:31:13,760
all these models coming out.

715
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:16,820
But none of them were really
things that are household names

716
00:31:16,820 --> 00:31:18,650
like Alpaca is today.

717
00:31:18,650 --> 00:31:21,770
Like, the team from
behind WizardLM

718
00:31:21,770 --> 00:31:24,530
where they created this method
called EvolInstruct, which

719
00:31:24,530 --> 00:31:26,220
is a synthetic data method--

720
00:31:26,220 --> 00:31:29,060
like, all these
things were clearly

721
00:31:29,060 --> 00:31:31,490
working for them based on the
models they were generating.

722
00:31:31,490 --> 00:31:33,470
But for whatever
reason, the narrative

723
00:31:33,470 --> 00:31:36,080
wasn't actually changed.

724
00:31:36,080 --> 00:31:38,630
There's some new data
sets from ultra--

725
00:31:38,630 --> 00:31:42,260
like, UltraLM is from an
open BNB in China that

726
00:31:42,260 --> 00:31:45,770
is releasing new data sets, more
people training on ShareGPT.

727
00:31:45,770 --> 00:31:49,370
The model called XwinLM
was the first one

728
00:31:49,370 --> 00:31:53,000
to be a similar
ballpark, and it's also

729
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:56,080
trained with RLHF, so not
just that carbon model.

730
00:31:56,080 --> 00:32:00,030
But for whatever reason, it's
like these didn't really splash.

731
00:32:00,030 --> 00:32:03,230
And that was this
summer after Llama 2,

732
00:32:03,230 --> 00:32:06,000
where fine-tuning
was chugging along.

733
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,812
But the narrative wasn't
changing all that much,

734
00:32:08,812 --> 00:32:10,020
at least from my perspective.

735
00:32:10,020 --> 00:32:11,500
But that's why I'm here.

736
00:32:11,500 --> 00:32:14,520

737
00:32:14,520 --> 00:32:16,440
But what was happening
in the background

738
00:32:16,440 --> 00:32:19,710
while the models weren't
seeming that different

739
00:32:19,710 --> 00:32:23,580
is that new evaluation tools
were coming out that ended up

740
00:32:23,580 --> 00:32:25,400
being the standard of today.

741
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:26,680
So you can see the dates here.

742
00:32:26,680 --> 00:32:31,210
So May 3, ChatBotArena, June 8,
AlpacaEval; June 22, MT Bench.

743
00:32:31,210 --> 00:32:34,120
Sometime in early July,
the Open LLM Leaderboard.

744
00:32:34,120 --> 00:32:36,930
All of these things were created
about the same time, where there

745
00:32:36,930 --> 00:32:39,630
was a desperate need to
get some sort of signal

746
00:32:39,630 --> 00:32:43,140
on what our fine-tuned
models are doing in the open.

747
00:32:43,140 --> 00:32:46,020
We don't have the
capability of paying humans

748
00:32:46,020 --> 00:32:48,120
to compare our
