00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:27,880
It's defining the biggest
language model players,

765
00:33:27,880 --> 00:33:30,760
like if Claude 3 is
better than GPT-4.

766
00:33:30,760 --> 00:33:34,980
But if I'm an engineer, A,
many small [INAUDIBLE] riders

767
00:33:34,980 --> 00:33:36,480
aren't going to get
their models in.

768
00:33:36,480 --> 00:33:38,190
And B, it takes--

769
00:33:38,190 --> 00:33:40,950
especially previously,
it used to take weeks

770
00:33:40,950 --> 00:33:42,420
to get your model's rating.

771
00:33:42,420 --> 00:33:43,900
But now it takes days.

772
00:33:43,900 --> 00:33:47,400
Like, I need to know what
my models are before I

773
00:33:47,400 --> 00:33:49,090
decide to actually release it.

774
00:33:49,090 --> 00:33:50,850
So that's the
biggest thing, where

775
00:33:50,850 --> 00:33:54,090
I know I need something
beyond ChatBotArena, just

776
00:33:54,090 --> 00:33:56,990
for my engineering development.

777
00:33:56,990 --> 00:33:58,460
And this is where
I like AlpacaEval

778
00:33:58,460 --> 00:33:59,940
and MT Bench really thrive.

779
00:33:59,940 --> 00:34:03,860
So AlpacaEval got-- slide
format and got changed,

780
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:07,550
but I'll just keep
rolling through this.

781
00:34:07,550 --> 00:34:10,100
AlpacaEval is the idea, if
you have a list of promts,

782
00:34:10,100 --> 00:34:14,510
that you compare to a
strong other base model,

783
00:34:14,510 --> 00:34:18,860
like OpenAI's
Davinci-003 or GPT-4.

784
00:34:18,860 --> 00:34:21,260
And then you ask a language
model which is better.

785
00:34:21,260 --> 00:34:23,270
And the data set
here is compiled

786
00:34:23,270 --> 00:34:27,860
from all these popular data sets
that I have been talking about

787
00:34:27,860 --> 00:34:31,969
so far, so data sets from
Open Assistant, Vicuna, Koala,

788
00:34:31,969 --> 00:34:32,810
Anthropic.

789
00:34:32,810 --> 00:34:35,909
All these data sets that
people have been using--

790
00:34:35,909 --> 00:34:37,949
they took the test
sets from those.

791
00:34:37,949 --> 00:34:39,980
And that's what
AlpacaEval mirrors.

792
00:34:39,980 --> 00:34:42,139
It's a known thing.

793
00:34:42,139 --> 00:34:46,260
It has some limitations because
there's only so many prompts.

794
00:34:46,260 --> 00:34:49,969
And it's like asking a language
model to provide a rating,

795
00:34:49,969 --> 00:34:51,530
is going to have
some ceiling where

796
00:34:51,530 --> 00:34:54,929
we don't know how to compare
two really good models.

797
00:34:54,929 --> 00:34:59,840
So it has more samples than
MT Bench, so there's more--

798
00:34:59,840 --> 00:35:02,300
so there's just
smaller error bars,

799
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:05,610
and it's easier to use because
it's a single-turn generation.

800
00:35:05,610 --> 00:35:08,880
But we've heard about the length
bias for a really long time,

801
00:35:08,880 --> 00:35:11,970
and it's not clear how to
interpret these top results.

802
00:35:11,970 --> 00:35:14,430
So this is an older
screenshot of the leaderboard.

803
00:35:14,430 --> 00:35:19,190
But what is beating a
model 95% of the time

804
00:35:19,190 --> 00:35:21,060
mean to another language model?

805
00:35:21,060 --> 00:35:23,060
That's the questions
that we can't really

806
00:35:23,060 --> 00:35:25,640
answer in the short term.

807
00:35:25,640 --> 00:35:30,590
AlpacaEval 2 came out, which
takes steps to this, where it--

808
00:35:30,590 --> 00:35:34,280
rate compares to GPT-4
rather than Davinci-003.

809
00:35:34,280 --> 00:35:36,780
Davinci-003 was an
InstructGPT variant.

810
00:35:36,780 --> 00:35:42,200
But at the end of
the day, GPT-4 is

811
00:35:42,200 --> 00:35:45,860
answering these questions in
the Alpaca style really well.

812
00:35:45,860 --> 00:35:49,730
So what does beating
GPT-4 exactly mean?

813
00:35:49,730 --> 00:35:52,340
And I think we need to get more
specific in our evaluations

814
00:35:52,340 --> 00:35:57,440
because I don't really know if I
care too much about a 20% or 30%

815
00:35:57,440 --> 00:36:00,750
score on AlpacaEval 2 because
I don't know what it means.

816
00:36:00,750 --> 00:36:02,810
And this is one this
is the opaqueness

817
00:36:02,810 --> 00:36:04,202
of all of our evaluations.

818
00:36:04,202 --> 00:36:05,660
We'll see this time
and time again,

819
00:36:05,660 --> 00:36:08,730
where we don't know what
an increase in score means.

820
00:36:08,730 --> 00:36:12,790
That's like the next step after
being able to do it easily.

821
00:36:12,790 --> 00:36:16,140
This update was pretty recent.

822
00:36:16,140 --> 00:36:18,930
MT Bench is pretty
similar, where,

823
00:36:18,930 --> 00:36:21,420
instead of comparing
to one model,

824
00:36:21,420 --> 00:36:23,040
you ask a language
model to provide

825
00:36:23,040 --> 00:36:25,240
a score to a list of prompts.

826
00:36:25,240 --> 00:36:27,030
So if I have a
model I'm training,

827
00:36:27,030 --> 00:36:30,090
I generate the completion
to 80 diverse prompts.

828
00:36:30,090 --> 00:36:33,180
And then I ask GPT-4,
hey, from 0 to 10,

829
00:36:33,180 --> 00:36:36,220
how good were each
of those completions?

830
00:36:36,220 --> 00:36:39,840
And this is good, but it
runs into the same problem

831
00:36:39,840 --> 00:36:42,670
of like, what if our model
is getting really good?

832
00:36:42,670 --> 00:36:46,380
If our model is getting
really good, it's just like--

833
00:36:46,380 --> 00:36:48,240
it becomes saturated.

834
00:36:48,240 --> 00:36:51,540
GPT-4 only gets to
about 9, and there's

835
00:36:51,540 --> 00:36:53,910
only about 80 prompts
in the evaluation set

836
00:36:53,910 --> 00:36:56,510
and all of these things.

837
00:36:56,510 --> 00:36:58,760
And one of the nuanced points
is that there's actually

838
00:36:58,760 --> 00:36:59,460
a variance.

839
00:36:59,460 --> 00:37:02,120
So even if you set
the temperature to 0,

840
00:37:02,120 --> 00:37:03,950
GPT-4 versions change.

841
00:37:03,950 --> 00:37:07,580
Your own generations from your
model you're trying to train

842
00:37:07,580 --> 00:37:08,390
can change.

843
00:37:08,390 --> 00:37:12,540
And this makes it better,
where it's like, OK,

844
00:37:12,540 --> 00:37:14,500
I can tell if a
model was really bad,

845
00:37:14,500 --> 00:37:17,250
if MT Bench and AlpacaEval
have really low scores.

846
00:37:17,250 --> 00:37:19,830
But it's hard to--

847
00:37:19,830 --> 00:37:23,890
still, we have this goal
for a precise evaluation.

848
00:37:23,890 --> 00:37:28,530
So in pretraining, we have
MT Bench and HellaSwag

849
00:37:28,530 --> 00:37:30,150
and all-- or sorry.

850
00:37:30,150 --> 00:37:33,630
In pretraining, we have MMLU and
HellaSwag and all these things

