942
00:41:43,367 --> 00:41:44,450
It turned out to be wrong.

943
00:41:44,450 --> 00:41:47,490

944
00:41:47,490 --> 00:41:49,770
I'm going to review
some fundamentals, just

945
00:41:49,770 --> 00:41:51,660
to make sure we're
talking the same language

946
00:41:51,660 --> 00:41:52,660
as we talk about this.

947
00:41:52,660 --> 00:41:55,120
And this will lead into direct
preference optimization.

948
00:41:55,120 --> 00:41:57,060
So there's a reason
why I'm doing math.

949
00:41:57,060 --> 00:41:59,280
I know this is not
a normal lecture,

950
00:41:59,280 --> 00:42:01,440
but here's the equation where--

951
00:42:01,440 --> 00:42:03,130
you'll see this in RLHF papers.

952
00:42:03,130 --> 00:42:06,830
This is what we're optimizing
when we're optimizing RLHF.

953
00:42:06,830 --> 00:42:09,150
It looks nebulous here.

954
00:42:09,150 --> 00:42:10,170
I'll break it down.

955
00:42:10,170 --> 00:42:13,340
So on the left side, we're
really maximizing with respect

956
00:42:13,340 --> 00:42:15,260
to some policy pi.

957
00:42:15,260 --> 00:42:18,680
This reward that is
parametrized by a network phi,

958
00:42:18,680 --> 00:42:22,130
and we're have a
penalty that is this KL

959
00:42:22,130 --> 00:42:25,790
term, which is the distance from
our policy to some reference.

960
00:42:25,790 --> 00:42:29,400
We want to increase reward, but
we want to constrain the model

961
00:42:29,400 --> 00:42:32,150
so that it doesn't-- this kind
of optimization doesn't go too

962
00:42:32,150 --> 00:42:34,040
far.

963
00:42:34,040 --> 00:42:36,770
And the primary
questions when doing this

964
00:42:36,770 --> 00:42:39,200
is, how do we implement
a good reward function?

965
00:42:39,200 --> 00:42:41,130
And how do we
optimize the reward?

966
00:42:41,130 --> 00:42:43,730
This is a really
RL-centric way of doing it,

967
00:42:43,730 --> 00:42:47,960
which is like, if you give me a
reward function, I can optimize.

968
00:42:47,960 --> 00:42:51,620
And the classic RL idea
was, I'm in an environment.

969
00:42:51,620 --> 00:42:54,290
That environment has the
reward function built in.

970
00:42:54,290 --> 00:42:57,480
In RLHF, we're designing
our own reward function.

971
00:42:57,480 --> 00:43:01,970
So this adds a lot of weirdness
to the actual optimization

972
00:43:01,970 --> 00:43:04,230
that we're doing.

973
00:43:04,230 --> 00:43:06,420
And what we do is--

974
00:43:06,420 --> 00:43:08,280
to get this reward
function is we

975
00:43:08,280 --> 00:43:10,750
learn what is called a
preference or a reward model.

976
00:43:10,750 --> 00:43:12,660
And the most popular
way to do this

977
00:43:12,660 --> 00:43:16,890
is to take a
language model that's

978
00:43:16,890 --> 00:43:20,340
predicting the separation
of two preferences.

979
00:43:20,340 --> 00:43:22,260
This is called a
Bradley-Terry model, which

980
00:43:22,260 --> 00:43:23,860
goes back to some economics.

981
00:43:23,860 --> 00:43:26,580
But the key idea
is that the reward

982
00:43:26,580 --> 00:43:29,040
will be proportional
to the probability

983
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:30,990
that the text they
have would be chosen

984
00:43:30,990 --> 00:43:33,270
over any other arbitrary text.

985
00:43:33,270 --> 00:43:34,980
[? Quickly ?] sounds
really theory like,

986
00:43:34,980 --> 00:43:37,920
but it outputs a scalar, which
is now a reward function.

987
00:43:37,920 --> 00:43:39,710
And it's based on
this pairwise data.

988
00:43:39,710 --> 00:43:42,300

989
00:43:42,300 --> 00:43:44,700
So the idea is,
with this equation,

990
00:43:44,700 --> 00:43:48,060
what if we just use gradient
ascent on this equation?

991
00:43:48,060 --> 00:43:51,690
And instead of trying to
learn a preference model

992
00:43:51,690 --> 00:43:53,760
and learn this R,
what if we just

993
00:43:53,760 --> 00:43:55,540
use gradient ascent directly?

994
00:43:55,540 --> 00:43:57,810
This is really what direct
preference optimization

995
00:43:57,810 --> 00:43:58,500
is doing.

996
00:43:58,500 --> 00:44:01,570
There's a bunch of math in
here to get what this R is.

997
00:44:01,570 --> 00:44:03,360
But this was
released back in May,

998
00:44:03,360 --> 00:44:05,140
so we've already
moved on months ahead.

999
00:44:05,140 --> 00:44:08,250
This chapter starts in
late September, October.

1000
00:44:08,250 --> 00:44:11,500
Back in May, when we're still
talking about OpenAssistant,

1001
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:13,170
this DPO paper came out.

1002
00:44:13,170 --> 00:44:14,593
It's a fantastic paper.

1003
00:44:14,593 --> 00:44:16,260
If you haven't read
it, it's a great way

1004
00:44:16,260 --> 00:44:18,100
to learn about
language model math.

1005
00:44:18,100 --> 00:44:19,960
It's worth reading.

1006
00:44:19,960 --> 00:44:24,090
But the core idea is like, why
are we spending all this time

1007
00:44:24,090 --> 00:44:27,300
learning a reward model when
we can just use gradient ascent

1008
00:44:27,300 --> 00:44:30,240
and solve for the loss function?

1009
00:44:30,240 --> 00:44:32,850
Some key ideas to
think about with DPO

1010
00:44:32,850 --> 00:44:36,430
is that DPO is extremely
simple to implement.

1011
00:44:36,430 --> 00:44:38,700
On the right here side
is the example code

1012
00:44:38,700 --> 00:44:42,460
from the DPO paper, where it's
like, as long as you have access

1013
00:44:42,460 --> 00:44:44,440
to the log probs
from a model, which

1014
00:44:44,440 --> 00:44:47,560
is a very core thing for
training language models,

1015
00:44:47,560 --> 00:44:49,900
you can compute the DPO loss.

1016
00:44:49,900 --> 00:44:51,940
Because of this, because
the loss function

1017
00:44:51,940 --> 00:44:55,180
is at a nice abstraction,
it scales nicely

1018
00:44:55,180 --> 00:44:56,680
with existing libraries.

1019
00:44:56,680 --> 00:45:00,910
And what it's actually doing
is training an implicit reward

1020
00:45:00,910 --> 00:45:01,430
function.

1021
00:45:01,430 --> 00:45:03,987
So the reward is a
function of the log probs.

1022
00:45:03,987 --> 00:45:06,070
I don't have the equation
here, because it quickly

1023
00:45:06,070 --> 00:45:07,900
becomes a rabbit hole.

1024
00:45:07,900 --> 00:45:13,900
But whatever the whole DPO
versus PPO debate means or--

1025
00:45:13,900 --> 00:45:16,540
or RPO, I don't even remember
what the paper, it's title

1026
00:45:16,540 --> 00:45:19,990
is, we're going to see
a lot of these things

1027
00:45:19,990 --> 00:45:21,860
because it's simple,
and it scales well.

1028
00:45:21,860 --> 00:45:24,520
That doesn't necessarily mean
the fundamental limits are

1029
00:45:24,520 --> 00:45:26,740
higher, but sometimes
it doesn't matter

1030
00:45:26,740 --> 00:45:29,440
if the limits are higher if
it's easier to make progress

1031
00:45:29,440 --> 00:45:31,600
on something because
it feels better

1032
00:45:31,600 --> 00:45:33,015
when progress is being made.

1033
00:45:33,015 --> 00:45:34,390
So that's really
a core thing, is

1034
00:45:34,390 --> 00:45:35,682
we'll keep seeing these models.

1035
00:45:35,682 --> 00:45:37,030
And we are.

1036
00:45:37,030 --> 00:45:39,160
And there's this
whole debate that

1037
00:45:39,160 --> 00:45:42,340
has gone on crushing a whole
bunch of these questions

1038
00:45:42,340 --> 00:45:46,180
by redirecting them in
a very political manner.

1039
00:45:46,180 --> 00:45:48,100
But it's like, should
we use REINFORCE?

1040
00:45:48,100 --> 00:45:51,330
What about other things?

1041
00:45:51,330 --> 00:45:54,490
They're very different
styles of optimization.

1042
00:45:54,490 --> 00:45:58,320
So in one half, we're
using RL update rules,

1043
00:45:58,320 --> 00:46:00,720
which is ultimately about
learning a value function

1044
00:46:00,720 --> 00:46:02,490
and then learning to update--

1045
00:46:02,490 --> 00:46:05,650
taking gradient steps with
respect to that value function.

1046
00:46:05,650 --> 00:46:07,920
In DPO we're taking
gradient steps directly

1047
00:46:07,920 --> 00:46:09,940
from the probabilities
of the language model.

1048
00:46:09,940 --> 00:46:12,400
They're very different
optimization regimes.

1049
00:46:12,400 --> 00:46:14,980
And there's this
great meme where all--

1050
00:46:14,980 --> 00:46:18,360
there was a month where the
whole NLP Twitter was just

1051
00:46:18,360 --> 00:46:19,170
arguing about this.

1052
00:46:19,170 --> 00:46:25,530
But both of them are continuing
to progress, and that is good.

1053
00:46:25,530 --> 00:46:27,680
Like, it will not just
be one or the other.

1054
00:46:27,680 --> 00:46:30,580

1055
00:46:30,580 --> 00:46:33,670
So what really made this
debate kick into gear

1056
00:46:33,670 --> 00:46:37,250
